{"text":"Recent research cautions against treating the latest interaction as definitive current intent. [Measuring Recency Bias (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09722) reports that excessive emphasis on recent items can hurt recommendation accuracy; [irrelevancy-skipping research (2025)](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neunet.2025.107134) notes that relevant historical interactions may be distant. [Intent diversification (2025)](https://doi.org/10.1145/3690624.3709429) also distinguishes diversity of user intent from simple item dissimilarity. Diagnostic implication: compare recent-window and full-history evidence, measure recommendation overlap and semantic repetition, and test recency weighting or truncation through controlled validation ablations. Similar titles alone cannot establish unwanted redundancy.

Complementarity and compatibility require separate judgments: an item may serve a complementary function while its technical fit remains unknown. For this task, a defensible probe would record the exact title/description evidence, relevant historical item, stated requirement, and any explicit contradiction; assign supported match, explicit conflict, or insufficient information. Missing specifications, ownership, and current demand remain unknown. This conservative design follows broader uncertainty research: the [2025 abstention survey](https://aclanthology.org/2025.tacl-1.26/) motivates withholding unsupported answers, while [AbstentionBench (2025)](https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/fb122bfc3f0127a94ded048b5b03496f-Abstract-Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track.html) shows that prompting alone does not solve uncertainty recognition.

Validate proposed failure labels against the twenty users’ feedback: report agreement, evidence coverage, abstention rate, and unsupported-conflict rate. Treat these as proposed diagnostics, not measured results; twenty users provide limited generalization. Keep simulator feedback distinct from observed human feedback. Select interventions using the fixed four-category macro validation HR@10, and examine whether recency interventions change both ranking performance and the diagnosed failures."}
